@ 29.1.1.1.42 - Proto-Athabaskan to Southern Tutchone
# citation: Whimemsz, from Krauss, Michael and Victor Golla (1981), “Northern Athapaskan Languages”. Handbook of North American Indians, Vol. 6 (Subarctic), 67 – 85 Most stem-final consonants lost, though some plain and labialized palatal reflexes have developed differently Acquisition of nasalized vowels and diphthongs /ɨ/ somehow develops
	{ P:[-voice]S, {P:[-voice]Š,P:[-voice]Šʷ} } > { P:[-voice]:[+laryngeal], P:[-voice]S }
	[+ cons, - fr, + bk, + hi, - lo] > P:[-voice]Š (although /xʲ/ remained as such in a few dialect͡s)
	[+click] > [+ cons, - fr, + bk, + hi, - lo]
	{ e, a } > { i, e }
	{ɑ,ə,ʊ} > {a,o}
	Vˀ > V:[+low tone]
	[+affricate] > F (some slight PO:[+affricate] changes; alveolars become dentals, for instance)